[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Backpressure Handling & Rate Limiting Algorithms"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to System Overload"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"In distributed systems, traffic spikes are inevitable. Without proper traffic management, downstream services can crash due to resource exhaustion (CPU, memory, database connections). Rate limiting and backpressure are two primary strategies used to maintain system stability under heavy loads."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Rate Limiting vs. Backpressure"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rate Limiting: Proactive control applied at the entry point of a system to restrict the number of requests a client can make in a given time window."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Backpressure: Reactive control propagated upstream from a struggling consumer, signaling producers to slow down their data emission rate."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm 1: Token Bucket"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Token Bucket algorithm allows a fixed capacity of tokens to be held in a bucket. Tokens are added at a constant rate. When a request arrives, a token must be consumed. If the bucket is empty, the request is either dropped or queued."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillRate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillRate = refillRate;
    this.lastRefilled = Date.now();
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefilled) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.refillRate);
    this.lastRefilled = now;
  }

  consume(tokens = 1) {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= tokens) {
      this.tokens -= tokens;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm 2: Leaky Bucket"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Leaky Bucket algorithm processes requests at a constant, fixed rate using a FIFO queue. If the queue overflows, incoming requests are dropped. This smooths out bursty traffic into a steady stream."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"class LeakyBucket {
  constructor(capacity, leakRatePerSec) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.queue = [];
    this.leakRate = leakRatePerSec;
    setInterval(() => this.leak(), 1000 / this.leakRate);
  }

  addRequest(request) {
    if (this.queue.length < this.capacity) {
      this.queue.push(request);
      return true;
    }
    return false; // Dropped
  }

  leak() {
    if (this.queue.length > 0) {
      const req = this.queue.shift();
      this.process(req);
    }
  }

  process(req) {
    console.log("Processing request: " + req);
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary & Best Practices"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Token Bucket when you want to allow short bursts of traffic while enforcing an average rate limit."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Leaky Bucket when you need a strictly regulated, smooth processing rate to protect fragile downstream dependencies."}]
